# Cloudflare edge headers for the static export.
#
# The load-bearing directive is connect-src. The user's OpenRouter key lives
# in this browser, so the realistic threat is script injection that exfiltrates
# it — and exfiltration needs a network destination. Restricting outbound
# connections to openrouter.ai means an injected script has nowhere to send it.
#
# script-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' because a static export cannot mint a
# per-request nonce and Next inlines its bootstrap. Blocking foreign script
# *origins* is still worth having; treat the inline allowance as the reason
# connect-src has to be strict rather than as a reason to relax it.
#
# `blob:` is in connect-src because images and attachments are held as object
# URLs (lib/blob.ts) and read back as bytes with fetch() whenever they leave
# the tab — a follow-up turn, an export, an image reference. `'self'` does not
# cover blob: URLs, so without it every one of those failed in production
# while working everywhere the headers were not applied. A blob: URL is local
# to this page; it is not a destination anything can be sent to.
#
# NOTE FOR THE R2 CUSTOM DOMAIN: serving ciphertext straight from R2 instead
# of through the Worker needs its origin added to connect-src as well as
# BLOB_PUBLIC_ORIGIN being set. `downloadBlob` fetches that URL with
# `mode: "cors"`, so without it the browser blocks the request before it is
# made and every image silently falls back to the billable Worker path — the
# optimisation would look like it had simply not worked.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' blob: https://openrouter.ai; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# Everything under /_next/static carries a content hash in its filename, so a
# changed file is a changed URL and a cached copy can never be stale. Without
# this they were served `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which made every repeat
# visit re-validate all nine entry scripts — nine conditional round trips
# before hydration, for bytes the browser already had.
#
# Scoped to the hashed directory on purpose: the HTML documents must keep
# revalidating, or a deploy would never reach anyone holding a cached page.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
